Problem statement
LLM Engineer Take-Home Assessment

Overview

Design and implement a containerized, production-grade multi-agent system with a self-improving evaluation loop, dynamic tool orchestration, and adversarial robustness testing. The system must demonstrate deep understanding of LLM internals, failure modes, and production constraints. Push everything to a public GitHub repository.

Task

1. Multi-Agent Orchestration with Dynamic Routing

Build a multi-agent pipeline where a master orchestrator agent dynamically decides at runtime which sub-agents to invoke, in what order, and with what context window budget. The orchestrator must not follow a hardcoded chain. Routing decisions must be made via structured reasoning and logged with justification.

Sub-agents required:

•⁠ ⁠A decomposition agent that breaks ambiguous queries into typed sub-tasks with explicit dependency graphs between them. Dependent sub-tasks must not execute until their dependencies resolve. •⁠ ⁠A retrieval-augmented agent that performs multi-hop reasoning across at least two retrieved chunks before forming an answer. Single-hop retrieval is not sufficient. The agent must cite which chunk contributed to which part of the answer. •⁠ ⁠A critique agent that reviews the output of every other agent, assigns a structured confidence score per claim, and flags specific spans of text it disagrees with, not the output as a whole. •⁠ ⁠A synthesis agent that merges outputs from all sub-agents, resolves contradictions flagged by the critique agent, and produces a final answer with a provenance map linking each sentence to its source agent and source chunk.

All inter-agent communication must pass through a shared context object with a defined schema. Agents must not call each other directly. The orchestrator mediates all handoffs.

2. Tool Calling with Failure Modes and Fallback Logic

Implement at least four tools with real tool-calling interfaces:

•⁠ ⁠A web search stub that returns structured results with source URLs and relevance scores •⁠ ⁠A code execution sandbox that runs Python snippets and returns stdout, stderr, and exit code •⁠ ⁠A structured data lookup tool that queries a local database via natural language converted to SQL by the agent •⁠ ⁠A self-reflection tool that the agent can call to re-read its own previous outputs within the session and identify contradictions

Each tool must have a defined failure contract: what it returns on timeout, on empty results, and on malformed input. The orchestrator must handle each failure mode differently. Fallback logic must be explicit in code, not embedded in a prompt instruction.

Tool calls must be logged with input, output, latency, and whether the agent accepted or rejected the tool output after receiving it. An agent that receives a tool result and decides it is insufficient must be able to re-call the tool with a modified input, up to two retries, with each retry logged separately.

3. Context Window Management

Implement a context budget manager that tracks token consumption per agent per turn. Each agent must declare its maximum context budget before execution. If an agent's assembled context exceeds its budget, the system must automatically summarize older context using a compression agent before proceeding. The compression must be lossless for structured data (tool outputs, scores, citations) and lossy only for conversational filler.

The context manager must expose a method that any agent can call to check remaining budget before adding to its context. Agents that ignore budget constraints and overflow must be caught and logged as a policy violation, not silently truncated.

4. Evaluation Pipeline with Adversarial Cases

Build an evaluation harness that runs 15 test cases through the full pipeline. The test set must include:

•⁠ ⁠Five straightforward queries with known correct answers for baseline scoring •⁠ ⁠Five queries with deliberately ambiguous or underspecified inputs to test decomposition quality •⁠ ⁠Five adversarial queries: prompt injections attempting to override agent instructions, queries containing factually confident but wrong premises, and queries designed to cause the critique agent to disagree with the synthesis agent and produce a contradiction that the system must resolve rather than surface to the user

Scoring must be multi-dimensional per test case: answer correctness, citation accuracy, contradiction resolution quality, tool selection efficiency (penalize unnecessary tool calls), context budget compliance, and critique agent agreement rate with final output.

Do not use a third-party eval framework as a black box. Build all scoring logic yourself. Each dimension must produce a numeric score with a written justification string, not just a number.

Every eval run must be stored in a database with full reproducibility: the exact prompt sent to each agent, the exact tool calls made, the exact outputs received, the scores, and the timestamp. Re-running the eval on the same inputs must produce a diff-able output so regressions are immediately visible.

5. Self-Improving Prompt Loop

After each eval run, implement a meta-agent that reads the failure cases, identifies the worst-performing prompt in the pipeline by dimension, and proposes a rewritten version of that prompt with a structured diff and justification. The proposed rewrite must be stored but not automatically applied. A separate endpoint must allow a human to approve or reject the rewrite. If approved, the system must re-run the eval on only the previously failed cases using the new prompt and log the delta in performance.

This loop must be auditable end to end. Every proposed rewrite, every approval or rejection, and every performance delta must be stored with timestamps and be queryable.

6. Streaming and Observability

All agent outputs must be streamed token by token to the client via Server-Sent Events. The client must be able to see which agent is currently writing, what tool calls are in flight, and the current context budget remaining, all in real time as the pipeline executes.

Implement structured logging throughout using a consistent schema: timestamp, agent ID, event type, input hash, output hash, latency, token count, and policy violations if any. Logs must be queryable. Add a single endpoint that returns a full execution trace for any job ID, reconstructing the exact sequence of agent decisions, tool calls, and handoffs in order.

7. API

Expose exactly five endpoints:

•⁠ ⁠One to submit a query and receive a streaming SSE response with real-time agent activity •⁠ ⁠One to retrieve the full execution trace for a completed job by job ID •⁠ ⁠One to retrieve the latest eval run summary broken down by test category and scoring dimension •⁠ ⁠One to submit a human approval or rejection for a pending prompt rewrite •⁠ ⁠One to trigger a targeted re-eval on previously failed cases using the latest approved prompts

All endpoints must be documented. Error responses must include a machine-readable error code, a human-readable message, and the job ID if applicable.

8. Containerization

Docker compose up must start every service with zero manual steps. Services required: the API server, a background worker that processes agent jobs asynchronously, the database, and a lightweight log query interface. Environment variables must be the only configuration mechanism. No credentials may be hardcoded anywhere in the repository.

9. GitHub Repository

The repository must include a README covering full setup instructions, architecture diagram as a text or image file, a description of every agent and its decision boundaries, known limitations with honest assessment of where the system breaks, what the self-improving loop does and does not do, and what you would build next.

